  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">AUD4m in general budget support was provided in recognition of SIG&#x2019;s progress against and commitment to the Joint Donor Review Group&#x2019;s (JPRG) Policy Reform Matrix (PRM). The PRM includes policy actions linked to public financial management, economic growth and resilience.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Technical support to the Ministry of Finance and Treasury&#x2019;s Procurement Unit &#x2013; such as the development of a procurement database, improved vetting and approvals through the Central tender Board &#x2013; reduced the backlog of procurement applications and reduced average turnaround times of procurement submissions by 76 percent.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Australia-Solomon Islands Partnership for Governance (ASIP-Gov) supported the Solomon Islands Ministry of Finance and Treasury (MoFT) to review its Debt Management Framework to enhance liability management in Solomon Islands, covering government guarantees, on-lending, sub-national borrowing, and direct borrowing by the Solomon Islands Government. ASIP-Gov also supported the creation of an Annual Borrowing Plan.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Through the Australia Solomon Islands Partnership for Governance, Australia provides technical advice to a range of Solomon Islands Government agencies and organisations to strengthen accountability and public financial management. For example: 
&#x200d;
With Australian support, MoFT adopted a new, risk-based approach to internal audit processes better aligned with international standards. NewTeamMate software was adopted providing a comprehensive platform for everything from risk assessment and planning to fieldwork, reporting, and follow-up. This led to greater implementation of audit recommendations and enhanced transparency across ministries. 
&#x200d;
The Office of the Auditor General, in partnership with the Audit Office of New South Wales, cleared provincial audit backlogs, enabling Provinces to access development funds and improving public accountability through published reports. 
&#x200d;
Through targeted support for women in leadership, DFAT funded training for six senior women leaders from State-Owned Enterprises and one government official, equipping them with governance and decision-making skills to influence board-level outcomes. The Solomon Islands Electoral Commission also introduced gender-equal recruitment tools and inclusive voter engagement campaigns, promoting broader civic participation. 
&#x200d;
Australia&#x2019;s support for the Institute of Solomon Islands Accountants enabled 21 public servants&#x2014;many of them women&#x2014; to complete a DFAT-supported Professional Diploma of Accounting which strengthened their public financial management and accounting skills.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">With Australian funding, UNDP provided technical support to the Solomon Islands Electoral Commission (SIEC) to develop a new Gender Equality and Social Inclusion policy and associated action plan in 2023. This policy resulted in a more inclusive 2024 election. For example, polling processes included accommodations for people with a disability and sign language interpreters were involved in announcement of election results and SIEC media conferences. Additionally, through the Australia Solomon Islands Partnership for Governance (ASIP-Gov), Australia supported Solomon Islands&#x2019; Ministry of Finance and Treasury (MoFT) to roll out a new Financial Management Information System (FMIS) across all Ministries and successfully develop its first Budget on the new system. This led to major efficiency gains compared to previous years. Through technical support we provided to the national payroll system, SIG was able process a Cost-of-Living Allowance adjustment and provide public servants with a pay rise.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Within the reporting period, Australia&#x2019;s advisers in the Ministry of Finance and Treasury provided targeted policy and technical advice to Solomon Islands to advance economic reform and strengthen public financial management. Through DFAT&#x2019;s support for a Debt Management Adviser, Solomon Islands developed its Annual Borrowing Plans and Medium-Term Debt Management Strategy, establishing clear frameworks for responsible borrowing.  Quarterly debt data is now regularly published. These reforms and actions contributed to a significant improvement in the IMF&#x2019;s assessment of Solmon&#x2019;s islands risk of external debt distress which was upgraded from &#x2018;moderate&#x2019; to &#x2018;low&#x2019; in 2025.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
